WEBVTT

00:00:00.103 --> 00:00:08.209
A USGS pine section records fires in 1723 and 1863 and death in 2007.

00:00:08.671 --> 00:00:13.704
Combine growth, scars and staining in a Pinus albicaulis life history.

00:00:14.158 --> 00:00:19.099
Assess drought, bark beetles and fungi without assuming one sole cause.

00:00:19.553 --> 00:00:25.605
One tree can preserve evidence of interacting fires, drought, insects and fungi.

00:00:26.066 --> 00:00:30.216
Read the combined history without assuming a single cause.

00:00:31.019 --> 00:00:34.402
Defoliation can alter growth and wood anatomy.

00:00:34.863 --> 00:00:39.429
Compare host and non-host trees, damage records and anatomy.

00:00:39.890 --> 00:00:44.534
Recovery after insect damage need not indicate canopy opening.

00:00:44.995 --> 00:00:48.067
Insects attack different organs and tissues.

00:00:48.521 --> 00:00:55.348
Combine width, anatomy, damage records and comparison trees to investigate their effects.

00:00:56.151 --> 00:01:00.251
Cockchafer damage can produce periodic narrow oak rings.

00:01:00.706 --> 00:01:04.949
Repetition can create apparent matches between different periods.

00:01:05.403 --> 00:01:10.022
Check anatomy, damage records and independent dating evidence.

00:01:10.482 --> 00:01:12.969
This periodic curve is fictional.

00:01:13.430 --> 00:01:17.943
Repetition alone can create an apparent match between different times and should not

00:01:17.956 --> 00:01:20.144
determine an alignment.

00:01:20.947 --> 00:01:24.869
Compare 49 oaks with 21 non-host ashes.

00:01:25.331 --> 00:01:29.239
Intra-annual density fluctuations (IADFs).

00:01:29.541 --> 00:01:32.629
Abrupt growth changes (AGC).

00:01:32.936 --> 00:01:37.390
The diagram summarizes the study design of the twenty-eighteen paper.

00:01:37.844 --> 00:01:43.884
Anatomical changes within rings are not uniquely diagnostic of insect damage.

00:01:44.687 --> 00:01:49.813
Compare pre- and post-infestation growth and anatomy in surviving hemlocks.

00:01:50.268 --> 00:01:54.985
Three of six sites declined; three showed no change or an increase.

00:01:55.439 --> 00:02:00.422
Do survivors represent the full population, including trees that died?.

00:02:00.729 --> 00:02:05.012
Surviving hemlocks showed different growth responses among sites.

00:02:05.466 --> 00:02:10.888
Consider changes in competition alongside other possible explanations.

00:02:11.691 --> 00:02:17.731
Hirano and Morimoto (1999) compared polluted and unpolluted stands.

00:02:18.193 --> 00:02:23.068
They assessed reduced growth alongside sulfur dioxide and a climate model.

00:02:23.522 --> 00:02:27.845
Read how comparisons and other evidence support the interpretation.

00:02:28.299 --> 00:02:33.391
This observational comparison uses climate modelling and pollution records alongside

00:02:33.404 --> 00:02:35.154
contrasting sites.

00:02:35.614 --> 00:02:39.884
It should not be mistaken for a simple controlled experiment.

00:02:40.687 --> 00:02:46.201
Damage and survival varied across the landscape after 18 May 1980.

00:02:46.663 --> 00:02:52.341
Dendrovolcanology examines suppression, scars, mortality and establishment.

00:02:52.795 --> 00:02:58.058
Compare deposits and topography; separate event dates from growth responses.

00:02:58.518 --> 00:03:02.689
An independently dated eruption helps study tree responses.

00:03:03.150 --> 00:03:09.024
Distinguish that task from using tree evidence to date an earlier unknown eruption.

00:03:09.827 --> 00:03:14.466
Dendroseismology investigates growth after root or crown damage.

00:03:14.921 --> 00:03:20.796
At Wrightwood, suppression from 1813 was linked to the late-1812 earthquake.

00:03:21.250 --> 00:03:26.612
Compare off-fault trees, sediments and historical records to assess causes.

00:03:27.066 --> 00:03:31.711
Earthquake timing and a growth response in the next year need not coincide.

00:03:32.171 --> 00:03:36.364
Compare off-fault trees and historical evidence as well.

00:03:37.167 --> 00:03:41.490
Release plus logging records: investigate thinning or removal.

00:03:41.946 --> 00:03:46.742
Host-specific suppression plus records: investigate insect damage.

00:03:47.196 --> 00:03:52.314
Scars, recruitment and archives: investigate postfire development.

00:03:52.775 --> 00:03:56.722
Different evidence can help compare competing explanations.

00:03:57.182 --> 00:04:01.544
Check how independent those evidence sources actually are.

00:04:02.347 --> 00:04:05.664
Persistent changes may be the signal of interest.

00:04:06.126 --> 00:04:09.724
Check whether detrending removes the change being studied.

00:04:10.178 --> 00:04:14.810
Keep raw series, processing choices and sensitivity checks.

00:04:15.270 --> 00:04:19.501
Persistent growth changes can be the target of disturbance research.

00:04:19.955 --> 00:04:24.924
Check that processing has not removed the variation being investigated.

00:04:25.727 --> 00:04:30.767
Specify the system, disturbance hypothesis and predicted evidence.

00:04:31.229 --> 00:04:35.650
Plan comparisons, sampling, dating and missing-data handling.

00:04:36.111 --> 00:04:40.492
Identify alternatives and evidence that could change the conclusion.

00:04:40.953 --> 00:04:46.690
Link the target, predicted observations, comparisons and additional evidence to produce a

00:04:46.703 --> 00:04:50.272
plan that can actually test the explanation.

00:04:51.075 --> 00:04:55.190
Does a 100% growth increase prove a canopy gap?.

00:04:55.498 --> 00:04:59.340
How do you treat no-scar years and non-recording periods?.

00:04:59.643 --> 00:05:05.406
Does a first ring dated 1980 at sampling height prove germination in 1980?.

00:05:05.709 --> 00:05:11.959
Review growth percentages, fire-scar denominators and tree-age interpretation, identifying

00:05:11.972 --> 00:05:15.140
the conditions needed for each conclusion.

00:05:15.943 --> 00:05:19.404
Lesson 7 introduces dendroclimatology.

00:05:19.866 --> 00:05:24.149
Examine species, site and seasonal climate responses.

00:05:24.603 --> 00:05:29.741
Keep competition, disturbance and insects in view as other influences.

00:05:30.195 --> 00:05:35.972
Next we reconstruct climate, testing relationships against instrumental observations while

00:05:35.985 --> 00:05:39.984
retaining the ecological alternatives discussed here.
